[REQ202503102209] - Canonical Project Structure  
A project may follow the [Canonical Project Structure](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2018/p1204r0.html).

## HAL
Someday I came across the HAL concept - making code independet from the underlying hardware. I wanted to learn more about it, so I am trying to learn about this concept with this project.

[REQ202609142152] -  HAL concept  
For the sake of reuseability the code shall be independet from the underlying hardware as much as possible.

## Header guards
Starting learning C coding, one of the first things are header guards. A header guard is usually used in *.h-files and it avoids multiple includes by the compiler of this file. 
```
#ifndef __LIBWEITWAPP_HAL_GPIO_H__
#define __LIBWEITWAPP_HAL_GPIO_H__

<CODE>

#endif // __LIBWEITWAPP_HAL_GPIO_H__
```

[REQ202609142156] - Header guard  
Each C/C++ header file shall include a header guard in order to avoid multiple includes by the compiler.

## File name in the first line of a file
Reading code example from books and other programmers I found that the usually the first line contained the filename itself as a comment. It seemed to be a convention. E.g.:
```
// main.c
...
```

[REQ202609142201] - File name in the first line of a file  
As a conevention the first line of a file shall contain a comment with the file name itself.

## Build options using #defines (HAL)
A HAl provides a common interface to vavrious underlying hardwares. Thus, the HAL implementation depends on the specific hardware used. During the build process the dedicated hardware needs to be choosen. To do so build flags are used and passed to the compiler, e.g. using the -D option (gcc).
e.g. LIBWEITWAPP_HAL_BUILD_ARDUINO

Mit platfromio wird dies in einem env so spezifiziert:
```
build_flags =
    -D BUILD_ENV
```

## Header guard for C/C++ compatibility
When trying my C code in combination with some C++ snipets for Arduino the code won't compile. I got an error like this:
```
/tmp/ccZ46vDy.ltrans0.ltrans.o: In function `main':
<artificial>:(.text.startup+0x8e): undefined reference to `hal_gpio_init(int, int)'
```
The solution is to place a so called C/C++ compatbility header guard in a C header-file (.h):
```
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif

<CODE>

#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif

```
